== 1. V1(c) of the note (Theorem 13.1(b3)) ==
  (a) 40 random pairs (A, P) of samplers, exact: Delta(B_P) = beta(A)/L in all 40, and Delta(B) <= beta(A)/L
      for all 64 tests B in all 40 (strictly below in 2514 of the 2560 cases)
  (b) 6 instances (4 hypotheses of weights 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16; the human is the third, K = 3), K0 = 3 with
      m = 20, 40 and K0 = 4 with m = 16, delta = 0.5, 0.1, 0.01; joint law of the recorded differences exact:
      instance 1, K0 = 3, m = 20: beta(A)/L = 0.2315; Pr[the bound of (c) fails] = 5.05e-07 (delta = 0.5), 1.57e-10 (delta = 0.1), 9.65e-15 (delta = 0.01)
      instance 1, K0 = 3, m = 40: beta(A)/L = 0.2315; Pr[the bound of (c) fails] = 2.88e-07 (delta = 0.5), 9.56e-11 (delta = 0.1), 6.45e-15 (delta = 0.01)
      instance 1, K0 = 4, m = 16: beta(A)/L = 0.2315; Pr[the bound of (c) fails] = 8.81e-10 (delta = 0.5), 1.34e-14 (delta = 0.1), 2.47e-18 (delta = 0.01)
      largest value of Pr[the bound of (c) fails]/delta over the 54 cases = 1.44e-06 (<= 1)
      one call per run, the class is the human alone (K0 = 0), P(1|x) = 1/4, A(1|x) = 3/4, m = 1, ..., 200, delta = 0.9, 0.5, 0.1, 0.01:
      largest value of Pr[the bound of (c) fails]/delta = 0.4861 (<= 1), at m = 1, delta = 0.9 (probability 0.4375)
  (c) 20 instances, each hypothesis, m = 10, 30, 100, 300, delta = 0.5, 0.1, 0.01: largest value of
      Pr[Delta(B_nu) - recorded difference >= sqrt(2 ln(1/(w(nu) delta))/m)]/(w(nu) delta) = 0.0140 (<= 1)
  (d) variables in {-1, 0, 1} on a grid of laws, m = 1, 2, 5, 20, 80, 100 values of t: largest value of
      Pr[mean <= expectation - t]/exp(-m t^2/2) = 0.9548 (<= 1), at Pr[+1] = 0.05, Pr[-1] = 0.95, m = 1, t = 0.10
  (e) an answerer that repeats its first answer at the second call, against a human who answers both calls with a fair coin (L = 2):
      Delta(B) = 0 for all 16 tests of pairs, and beta(A)/L = 1/4: the identity of (a) needs an imitator that looks at the input only

== 2. R7 (Proposition 5.6) when no hypothesis fits the recorded answers ==
  class of three functions; human: a sampler outside the class; when no hypothesis fits: the mixture under the prior weights
    n = 8: Pr[no hypothesis fits the data at the start of round n] = 0.7563; largest sum over the rounds, over the 7 sets X0, = 2.5525 <= L = 3;
    so R7: 0.3191 <= L/n = 0.3750; three singletons at once: 0.6366 <= 3 L/n = 1.1250
  class of three functions; human: a sampler outside the class; when no hypothesis fits: always the answer 1
    n = 8: Pr[no hypothesis fits the data at the start of round n] = 0.7563; largest sum over the rounds, over the 7 sets X0, = 2.4890 <= L = 3;
    so R7: 0.3111 <= L/n = 0.3750; three singletons at once: 0.6628 <= 3 L/n = 1.1250
  class of three functions; human: a sampler outside the class; when no hypothesis fits: a rule that depends on the data
    n = 8: Pr[no hypothesis fits the data at the start of round n] = 0.7563; largest sum over the rounds, over the 7 sets X0, = 2.5405 <= L = 3;
    so R7: 0.3176 <= L/n = 0.3750; three singletons at once: 0.6458 <= 3 L/n = 1.1250
  class of three functions; human: a process that depends on the round and the data; when no hypothesis fits: the mixture under the prior weights
    n = 8: Pr[no hypothesis fits the data at the start of round n] = 0.7400; largest sum over the rounds, over the 7 sets X0, = 2.5785 <= L = 3;
    so R7: 0.3223 <= L/n = 0.3750; three singletons at once: 0.6353 <= 3 L/n = 1.1250
  class of three functions; human: a process that depends on the round and the data; when no hypothesis fits: always the answer 1
    n = 8: Pr[no hypothesis fits the data at the start of round n] = 0.7400; largest sum over the rounds, over the 7 sets X0, = 2.5112 <= L = 3;
    so R7: 0.3139 <= L/n = 0.3750; three singletons at once: 0.6580 <= 3 L/n = 1.1250
  class of three functions; human: a process that depends on the round and the data; when no hypothesis fits: a rule that depends on the data
    n = 8: Pr[no hypothesis fits the data at the start of round n] = 0.7400; largest sum over the rounds, over the 7 sets X0, = 2.5647 <= L = 3;
    so R7: 0.3206 <= L/n = 0.3750; three singletons at once: 0.6432 <= 3 L/n = 1.1250
  control (a fair coin added to the class, so that some hypothesis always fits): Pr[no hypothesis fits] = 0.0; largest sum = 2.5439 <= 3
  largest ratio of a sum to L in the six cases with a class that can fail to fit: 0.8595 (<= 1)

== 3. Numbers of the section text ==
  Section 3.1: L = 10, K = 1e4, 0.1 unfaithful calls per run: n = L K ln 2/0.1 = 6.931e+05 answers; at one hour each, 346.6 working years of 2000 hours
  Section 3.1 (finding F21): L K ln 2/n is below the trivial bound L on the number of unfaithful calls only for n > K ln 2, and below 1,
    as a bound on the probability of an unfaithful run, only for n > L K ln 2; for K = 100, L = 10 these are n > 69.3 and n > 693.1
  Section 3.3, R7 in bits: n = 1e5, L = 10: log2(n/L) = 13.29; for delta = 0.1, log2(delta n/L) = 9.97;
    a prefix-free language has at most 2^b programs of at most b bits: 2^9 L/n = 0.0512 <= 0.1 < 2^10 L/n = 0.1024; 2^13 L/n = 0.82, 2^14 L/n = 1.64
    (all binary strings as programs: 1023 strings have fewer than 9.97 bits, and 1023 L/n = 0.1023 > 0.1: the count 2^b needs a prefix-free language, or a whole number b)
  Section 3.4, R3 for a person: K = 1e9, theta = 0.1, excess failure 0.1: K ln 2/(theta delta) = 6.93e+10 answers = 3.47e+07 working years at an hour each;
    for a human who varies, K ln 2/(2 theta^2 delta^2) = 3.47e+12 answers
  Section 3.4, the sentence on N8: with g critical steps in 2k + 1 copies each, L = g (2k + 1) and theta = (k + 1)/L; theta g = 1 (k = 0), 2/3 (k = 1), 6/11 (k = 5), 101/201 (k = 100): between 1/2 and 1
  Section 3.5: m = 300 rounds without a recorded disagreement certify, at delta = 0.05, beta(A) <= L ln(20)/300 = 0.00999 L;
    by R3 with k + 1 >= theta L and theta = 0.1 the excess failure is at most 0.0999
  Section 3.5, V1(c) for a person: the term sqrt(2 (K0 ln 2 + ln(1/delta))/m) is below 1 only for m > 2 K0 ln 2 = 1.39e+09 when K0 = 1e9
